#{[17592186543502 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543502 #edb/ref 1069 "q-100242" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543502 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543502 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{ex}{5})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543502 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d345e50-4921-588f-ae74-039f20e6e137" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543502 #edb/ref 1074 "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule and then the product rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{ex}{5}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ex)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543519 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543519 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47198" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543519 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543519 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{z}^{6}}{13})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543519 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a291781-59c8-57ab-aafe-d8f0841ec267" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543519 #edb/ref 1074 "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient quotient and then the power rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{z}^{6}}{13}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{6})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(13) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(13).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543524 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543524 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79453" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543524 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543524 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${log}_{5}⁡(x)=3$ and ${log}_{5}⁡(y)=2$, then ${log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543524 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "841f3191-5c7e-5e47-b725-127033cb9a73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543524 #edb/ref 1074 "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that ${log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3}) & ={log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2})+{log}_{5}⁡({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =2{log}_{5}⁡(x)+3{log}_{5}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${log}_{5}⁡(x)=3$ and ${log}_{5}⁡(y)=2$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2{log}_{5}⁡(x)+3{log}_{5}⁡(y)=2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 2=12.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})=12.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543526 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543526 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66781" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543526 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543526 #edb/ref 1071 "${log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{27})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543526 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8b91269e-ac3e-5858-87ab-575f9e7b0df8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543526 #edb/ref 1074 "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule and then the product rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{27}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(5x)-{log}_{3}⁡(27) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(5)+{log}_{3}⁡(x)-{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{3}) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(5)+{log}_{3}⁡(x)-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543531 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543531 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98925" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543531 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543531 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${log}_{2}⁡(x)=k$ and ${log}_{2}⁡(y)=4$, then ${log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543531 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9796dfce-fb6b-57bd-b442-e9278daaaa20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543531 #edb/ref 1074 "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that ${log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3}) & ={log}_{2}⁡(x)+{log}_{2}⁡({y}^{3}) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(x)+3{log}_{2}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${log}_{2}⁡(x)=k$ and ${log}_{2}⁡(y)=4,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+3{log}_{2}⁡(y) & =k+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =k+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})=k+12.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543541 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543541 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1110" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543541 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543541 #edb/ref 1071 "Express $\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(x)-5{log}_{5}⁡(y)$ as a single logarithm." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543541 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a8ef19a1-f2fa-5f29-a4ae-a1f3f6bb227d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543541 #edb/ref 1074 "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the power rule and then the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(x)-5{log}_{5}⁡(y) & ={log}_{5}⁡({x}^{1/2})-{log}_{5}⁡({y}^{5}) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\sqrt{x})-{log}_{5}⁡({y}^{5}) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{5}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543573 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543573 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543573 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543573 #edb/ref 1071 "${log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{\\sqrt{z}})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543573 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e3b649a8-d282-53b7-b8c8-e883fd47c0b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543573 #edb/ref 1074 "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{\\sqrt{z}})={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})-{log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z})\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})-{log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z})={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})+{log}_{3}⁡({y}^{3})-{log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z}).\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})+{log}_{3}⁡({y}^{3})-{log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z})=2{log}_{3}⁡(x)+3{log}_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z).\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{\\sqrt{z}})=2{log}_{3}⁡(x)+3{log}_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543577 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543577 #edb/ref 1069 "q-100243" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543577 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543577 #edb/ref 1071 "Express $\\frac{1}{3}{log}_{4}⁡(x)-{log}_{4}⁡(y)$ as a single logarithm." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543577 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e70f2288-3b25-5d12-a8c5-361a6e66e0f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543577 #edb/ref 1074 "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the power rule and then the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{log}_{4}⁡(x)-{log}_{4}⁡(y) & ={log}_{4}⁡({x}^{1/3})-{log}_{4}⁡(y) \\\\  & ={log}_{4}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{x})-{log}_{4}⁡(y) \\\\  & ={log}_{4}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x}}{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
